<section class="block" id="situation">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="kicker">01 — The Situation</span>
    <h2>A business that looked successful from the outside, and felt unsustainable from the inside.</h2>

    <p>Jasmine had real clients, real demand, and an audience that trusted her. What she did not have was a reason for any of her prices. They had settled over time, rather than being decided.</p>

    <div class="pull-line">She could tell you what she charged. She could not tell you why.</div>

    <p>She was managing a small team to deliver the visible parts of her business, while running the invisible parts almost entirely by herself, the work that built her credibility and kept her pipeline warm. The split was backwards. The work taking most of her week was consistently the work producing the least of her income.</p>

    <p>She could not say, with any confidence, what a single client actually cost her to serve. That meant she could not say, with any confidence, whether a client was worth keeping at the rate she had agreed to.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="built">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="kicker">02 — What Was Built</span>
    <h2>Six weeks. Four systems. Nothing left to guesswork.</h2>

    <p>The engagement ran as a structured Revenue Operating System, ten sessions across four phases: diagnosis, design, implementation, and handoff. Four systems came out of it.</p>

    <div class="system-list">
      <div class="system-item">
        <span class="system-mark">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>A Cost of Delivery Framework</h3>
          <p>Every service she offered was repriced against what it actually costs to deliver, not what felt reasonable at the time. Pricing stopped being a feeling and became a calculation she could defend to anyone.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="system-item">
        <span class="system-mark">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>A Rebuilt Offer Architecture</h3>
          <p>Her flagship offer went from a list of features with a price attached, to a structure where every component carried its own market value before the offer price was ever named.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="system-item">
        <span class="system-mark">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>A Client Acquisition System</h3>
          <p>A qualification step, a rule for reserving her time, and a rhythm for asking for referrals, solving the quiet constraint that was costing her clients before pricing ever entered the conversation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="system-item">
        <span class="system-mark">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>A Financial Operating System</h3>
          <p>A live, monthly picture of where her money goes and what she actually keeps, replacing a business run on instinct with one she can see clearly, every month.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="changed">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="kicker">03 — What Changed</span>
    <h2>Not a feeling. A number she can point to.</h2>

    <div class="stat-block">
      <div class="stat-number">+40%</div>
      <p class="stat-caption">Once the system made clear exactly what her business was producing, we were able to justify a pricing increase that grew her monthly income by 40 percent.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="outcomes">
      <div class="outcome">
        <span class="outcome-tag">Pricing</span>
        <span class="outcome-text">Onboarded a new client at a rate meaningfully above anything she had charged before, and defended every line of it without hesitation.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="outcome">
        <span class="outcome-tag">Delegation</span>
        <span class="outcome-text">Fully handed off one of the services that used to consume the most hours on her calendar. It now runs on a real margin, without her touching the execution.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="outcome">
        <span class="outcome-tag">Focus</span>
        <span class="outcome-text">Narrowed her attention to the two offers producing most of her revenue, and let the rest either get systemised or fall away.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="outcome">
        <span class="outcome-tag">Momentum</span>
        <span class="outcome-text">Closed a second client at a higher rate in the weeks that followed, using the same framework, without needing to build anything new for the conversation.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="testimonial-block">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="kicker">In Her Words</span>

    <p class="big-quote">Now if someone asks me how much I charge for LinkedIn brand management, I say it without flinching.</p>

    <div class="quote-attribution">
      <img class="quote-avatar" src="assets/jasmine-photo.png" alt="Jasmine Gathoga">
      <div>
        <div class="name">Jasmine Gathoga</div>
        <div class="role">LinkedIn Brand Strategist &amp; Designer &middot; Canvassador &middot; Canva Creator</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="secondary-quotes">
      <p class="secondary-quote"><span class="quote-mark">&ldquo;</span>I kept working and working and working and still not seeing any progress in any way, because I was working for less than most of my competitors, and nowhere near the global market. But how do I price for a global market when I don't even know what goes into it?</p>

      <p class="secondary-quote"><span class="quote-mark">&ldquo;</span>He helped me understand my working rate, how to calculate my expenses, what to consider before ever stating a price. Then he helped me build an offer I could defend, line by line, one that actually looks irresistible, and has helped me close clients at a much higher rate than before.</p>

      <p class="secondary-quote"><span class="quote-mark">&ldquo;</span>If you don't know how you're spending, you can't know how much you're making, and you can't manage your business wisely. He's not just talk. He is talk and action, with a lot of amazing experience and wisdom to guide you as a professional. Do it 101%. You will not regret it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

